The resulting text is again processed for macro substitutions.
.It Ic @ifelse No "" Bro :repeat Ar ARG Ns :string! Brc Ic @
.Ic @ifelse@
takes 3 or more arguments.
If the first argument is equal to the second, then the value is the
third argument.
If not equal, and if there are
.Em more
than four arguments, then the
process repeats with arguments 4, 5, 6, and 7.
Otherwise (either three or four arguments), the value is either the fourth argument if present, or null
if omitted.
The resulting text is subject to macro expansion.
Long lines may be split using
.Ic @\e .
This baroque arrangement is modeled after
.Xr m4 1 .
For a saner syntax, try the
.Ic @case
or
.Ic @if
commands.
However,
.Ic @ifelse@
is a function, not a command, so it can be used
in-line with other text, whereas
.Ic @if
and
.Ic @case
cannot.
.It Ic @ifx No "" Bro Ar EXPR Ns :string Ar TRUE_TEXT Ns :string! :optional Ar FALSE_TEXT Ns :string! Brc Ic @
Evaluate
.Va EXPR ;
if True, return
.Va TRUE_TEXT
else return
.Va FALSE_TEXT .
The
.Va FALSE_TEXT
argument is optional.
The resulting text is again processed for macro substitutions.
.Ic @ifx
stands for
.Dq IF eXpression .
